    {
      "@id": "https://inis.iaea.org/records/p0nd1-d5c88",
      "@type": "dcmitype:Text",
      "dcterms:identifier": [
        "https://inis.iaea.org/records/p0nd1-d5c88",
        "IWGFPT-32",
        "INIS RN:21003206"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "The diffusion coefficient for fission gas atoms in uranium dioxide",
      "dcterms:description": "A re-appraisal of the diffusion coefficient for xenon and krypton atoms in uranium dioxide is made based on recent data published in the open literature. In particular the application to high burn-up fuel is considered. A re-evaluation is made of experiments carried out at AERE Harwell and reported by Turnbull, Friskney, Findley, Johnson and Walter, (J. Nucl. Mat. vol. 107, nos 2 and 3, June 1982), and the impact of this on the application of a revised diffusion coefficient is discussed. In particular with a mechanistic approach the release of fission gases can only be correlated with the product (S/V) * √D, where S/V is the specific surface area and D is the diffusion coefficient. The paper shows that when applied to both stable and radioactive fission gas release measurements from non-interlinked fuel, the revised values of D give values of S/V in better agreement with calculated geometric values than previously obtained. The paper continues by calculating values of the diffusion coefficient from out-of-pile annealing studies on fuel covering a range of burn-up. The values obtained at high burn-up are compared with values deduced from the elegant EPMA measurements performed on retained xenon in power ramped fuel rods published by Walker and Lassmann, (J. Nucl. Mat. vol. 138, 1986). (author). ",
      "dcterms:format": "application/pdf",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "J. A. Turnbull",
        "R. J. White",
        "C. Wise"
      ],
      "dcterms:publisher": "International Atomic Energy Agency",
      "dcterms:bibliographicCitation": [
        "Turnbull, J. A., et al. (1988). The diffusion coefficient for fission gas atoms in uranium dioxide. IWGFPT-32, Preston, UK, Sep 18-22."
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "1988",
      "dcterms:accessRights": "Restricted Access",
      "neo:referenceINIS": [
        "URANIUM DIOXIDE",
        "FISSION PRODUCTS",
        "DIFFUSION",
        "XENON",
        "NUCLEAR FUELS"
      ],
      "dcterms:subject": [
        "fission gas diffusion coefficient",
        "intragranular diffusion",
        "athermal and thermal diffusion regimes"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-3115(71)90075-4",
      "@type": "dcmitype:Text",
      "dcterms:identifier": [
        "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-3115(71)90075-4",
        "doi:10.1016/0022-3115(71)90075-4"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "The re-solution of fission-gas atoms from bubbles during the irradiation of UO2 at an elevated temperature",
      "dcterms:description": "Uranium dioxide irradiated at a low temperature was annealed to form a uniform distribution of fission-gas bubbles. Some specimens were re-irradiated to a low dose at 1200 °C and the bubble distributions compared with and without this second irradiation. The enrichments of the starting materials were chosen such that specimens received two different doses for the same neutron exposure. Whereas in the higher-dose specimens the original bubble population was completely dispersed, the lower-dose specimens contained remnants of the original bubbles. From these observations an evaluation has been made of the efficiency of the re-solution process. The distribution of fission-gas during irradiation is discussed in the light of the present results.",
      "dcterms:format": "application/pdf",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "J. A. Turnbull"
      ],
      "dcterms:publisher": "Elsevier",
      "dcterms:bibliographicCitation": [
        "Turnbull, J. A. (1971). The re-solution of fission-gas atoms from bubbles during the irradiation of UO2 at an elevated temperature. Journal of Nuclear Materials, 38, 203."
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "1971",
      "dcterms:accessRights": "Restricted Access",
      "neo:referenceINIS": [
        "URANIUM DIOXIDE",
        "FISSION PRODUCTS",
        "BUBBLES",
        "IRRADIATION",
        "NUCLEAR FUELS"
      ],
      "dcterms:subject": [
        "fission gas re-solution",
        "irradiation-induced re-solution rate",
        "intragranular bubbles"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-3697(58)90053-2",
      "@type": "dcmitype:Text",
      "dcterms:identifier": [
        "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-3697(58)90053-2",
        "doi:10.1016/0022-3697(58)90053-2"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Theory of diffusion-limited precipitation",
      "dcterms:description": "A simple theory for diffusion-limited general precipitation from a supersaturated solution upon an array of particles is described. We calculate the time-dependence of the unprecipitated fraction of the excess solute for small spherical, spheroidal, and cylindrical particles. This fraction has the form exp(—btn) for small t. If the particles grow from initially negligible dimensions with constant eccentricity, and the growth is entirely diffusion-limited, we find n=32 for all spheroids, including rods and disks as well as spheres. This contrasts with earlier evaluations, which gave n=52 for disks and n = 2 for rods and which are incorrect. We find n = 1 for cylindrical particles. Also, n is 1 for small disks and rods, of finite initial dimensions, that remain highly eccentric and do not alter their longer dimensions appreciably during growth. Regardless of particle shape, the unprecipitated fraction is given approximately by [G exp(—at)] when the fraction is less than 12. We also give an exact solution of the time-dependent diffusion equation for the growth of a spheroidal particle in an infinite medium, and we show that under strictly diffusion-limited conditions the particle grows with constant eccentricity and that its dimensions are proportional to √t.",
      "dcterms:format": "application/pdf",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "F. S. Ham"
      ],
      "dcterms:publisher": "Elsevier",
      "dcterms:bibliographicCitation": [
        "Ham, F. S. (1958). Theory of diffusion-limited precipitation. Journal of Physics and Chemistry of Solids, 6, 335-351."
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "1958",
      "dcterms:accessRights": "Restricted Access",
      "neo:referenceINIS": [
        "DIFFUSION",
        "PRECIPITATION",
        "MATHEMATICAL MODELS",
        "NUCLEATION"
      ],
      "dcterms:subject": [
        "diffusion-limited precipitation",
        "trapping rate",
        "sink strength",
        "Ham's theory"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnucmat.2006.03.010",
      "@type": "dcmitype:Text",
      "dcterms:identifier": [
        "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnucmat.2006.03.010",
        "doi:10.1016/j.jnucmat.2006.03.010"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Re-solution of fission gas - A review: Part I. Intragranular bubbles",
      "dcterms:description": "Theories of fission-fragment-driven re-solution of fission-gas atoms from intragranular bubbles in irradiated UO2 nuclear fuel are reviewed. Two mechanisms of re-solution are generally accepted: the heterogeneous process destroys entire bubbles in the path of fission fragments and returns the gas to the solid as individual atoms; the homogeneous process re-solves fission-gas atoms singly by scattering collisions with fission fragments and uranium recoils whose paths intersect the bubbles. Coupling of these two re-solution models with the bubble nucleation analogs determines the size and number density of the intragranular bubble population. Two approaches are reviewed: the single-size theory, in which all bubbles are accorded one size, and the bubble distribution theory, which seeks to determine the variation of bubble number density with size.",
      "dcterms:format": "application/pdf",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "D. R. Olander",
        "D. Wongsawaeng"
      ],
      "dcterms:publisher": "Elsevier",
      "dcterms:bibliographicCitation": [
        "Olander, D. R., & Wongsawaeng, D. (2006). Re-solution of fission gas - A review: Part I. Intragranular bubbles. Journal of Nuclear Materials, 354, 94-109."
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2006",
      "dcterms:accessRights": "Restricted Access",
      "neo:referenceINIS": [
        "FISSION PRODUCTS",
        "BUBBLES",
        "NUCLEATION",
        "REVIEWS",
        "NUCLEAR FUELS"
      ],
      "dcterms:subject": [
        "fission gas re-solution",
        "bubble nucleation rate",
        "intragranular bubbles",
        "review"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-3115(79)90035-7",
      "@type": "dcmitype:Text",
      "dcterms:identifier": [
        "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-3115(79)90035-7",
        "doi:10.1016/0022-3115(79)90035-7"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Grain-boundary diffusion in uranium dioxide: The correlation between sintering and creep and a reinterpretation of creep mechanism",
      "dcterms:description": "Previously published data on the final stage sintering kinetics of stoichiometric uranium dioxide are correlated with a reinterpretation of low-stress creep behaviour of identical material (data on both processes by the present authors). For both processes the rate-controlling diffusional flux is considered to be that of uranium ions along grain boundaries. The effective diffusion coefficient for uranium ion diffusion along grain boundaries is estimated. Comparisons are made between this value and those previously measured by radio-tracer methods.",
      "dcterms:format": "application/pdf",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "A. B. Reynolds",
        "C. Burton"
      ],
      "dcterms:publisher": "Elsevier",
      "dcterms:bibliographicCitation": [
        "Reynolds, A. B., & Burton, C. (1979). Grain-boundary diffusion in uranium dioxide: The correlation between sintering and creep and a reinterpretation of creep mechanism. Journal of Nuclear Materials, 82, 22-25."
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "1979",
      "dcterms:accessRights": "Restricted Access",
      "neo:referenceINIS": [
        "URANIUM DIOXIDE",
        "GRAIN BOUNDARIES",
        "DIFFUSION",
        "SINTERING",
        "CREEP"
      ],
      "dcterms:subject": [
        "grain-boundary diffusion",
        "vacancy diffusivity",
        "sintering-creep correlation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2021.10.028",
      "@type": "dcmitype:Text",
      "dcterms:identifier": [
        "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2021.10.028",
        "doi:10.1016/j.net.2021.10.028"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "On the use of spectral algorithms for the prediction of short-lived volatile fission product release: Methodology for bounding numerical error",
      "dcterms:description": "Recent developments on spectral diffusion algorithms, i.e., algorithms which exploit the projection of the solution on the eigenfunctions of the Laplacian operator, demonstrated their effective applicability in fast transient conditions. Nevertheless, the numerical error introduced by these algorithms, together with the uncertainties associated with model parameters, may impact the reliability of the predictions on short-lived volatile fission product release from nuclear fuel. In this work, we provide an upper bound on the numerical error introduced by the presented spectral diffusion algorithm, in both constant and time-varying conditions, depending on the number of modes and on the time discretization. The definition of this upper bound allows introducing a methodology to a priori bound the numerical error on short-lived volatile fission product retention.",
      "dcterms:format": "application/pdf",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9139-2454",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2256-8409",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9754-4535"
      ],
      "dcterms:publisher": "Elsevier",
      "dcterms:bibliographicCitation": [
        "Zullo, G., Pizzocri, D., & Luzzi, L. (2022). On the use of spectral algorithms for the prediction of short-lived volatile fission product release: Methodology for bounding numerical error. Nuclear Engineering and Technology, 54, 1195-1205. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2021.10.028"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2022",
      "dcterms:accessRights": "Open Access",
      "dcterms:license": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/",
      "neo:referenceINIS": [
        "FISSION PRODUCT RELEASE",
